WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.040
The C memory model includes rules for shared memory between threads.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:14.500
A data race occurs when multiple threads access the same memory location concurrently, at least one access modifies it, and the accesses are not properly synchronized.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.120
Data races can produce undefined behavior.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.140
Mutexes and other synchronization mechanisms protect shared state and establish ordering.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:31.080
They are often the clearest choice when a shared invariant involves more than one object, more than one field, or a sequence of operations that must be understood together.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:37.220
_Atomic types and atomic operations provide language-level tools for shared data when used correctly.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:46.000
Atomics can be useful for counters, flags, references, and carefully designed coordination, but they do not remove the need to define invariants and ownership.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:51.740
Atomic access to one variable does not automatically make a larger data structure safe.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.800
Memory order controls how operations are ordered around atomic accesses and should be chosen deliberately.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:04.180
Stronger ordering can be easier to reason about, while weaker ordering requires careful proof of correctness.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.920
Teams should prefer designs that reviewers can explain and maintain.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.140
volatile is not a general-purpose thread synchronization mechanism.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.760
It may be relevant for certain special interactions, but it should not be used as a substitute for atomics or locks.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:25.620
Concurrent C should be designed around ownership, invariants, synchronization policy, and reviewable shared-state rules.
